{
  "id": "try-except",
  "title": "Try / Except",
  "exercises": [
    {
      "id": "01",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Catching a Bad Number",
      "sidebar_tag": "try/except",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a line of text and try to convert it to an integer with",
        "<code>int()</code>, then print it. If the conversion raises a",
        "<code>ValueError</code> (because the text isn't a valid whole",
        "number), print <code>Invalid number</code> instead.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "42",
        "42</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "abc",
        "Invalid number</pre>",
        "<p>A <code>try</code> block lets you attempt something that might",
        "fail. If it raises the exception type named in the matching",
        "<code>except</code> block, Python jumps straight there instead of",
        "crashing the whole program.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: try converting s to an int and printing it.",
        "# If that raises a ValueError, print \"Invalid number\" instead."
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "42"
          ],
          "expected": "42"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "abc"
          ],
          "expected": "Invalid number"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "-7"
          ],
          "expected": "-7"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "12.5"
          ],
          "expected": "Invalid number"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "02",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Catching a Division by Zero",
      "sidebar_tag": "except",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read two integers <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>. Try printing",
        "<code>a / b</code>. If <code>b</code> is 0, Python raises a",
        "<code>ZeroDivisionError</code> &mdash; catch it and print",
        "<code>Cannot divide by zero</code> instead.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "10",
        "2",
        "5.0</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "10",
        "0",
        "Cannot divide by zero</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "a = int(input())",
        "b = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: try printing a / b.",
        "# If that raises a ZeroDivisionError, print \"Cannot divide by zero\" instead."
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "5.0"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Cannot divide by zero"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "9",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "3.0"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "7",
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Cannot divide by zero"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "03",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "The else Clause",
      "sidebar_tag": "try/except/else",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a line of text and try converting it to an integer. If it",
        "fails, print <code>Invalid number</code> as before &mdash; but if it",
        "<strong>succeeds</strong>, use an <code>else</code> block to print",
        "<code>Valid number: &lt;n&gt;</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "42",
        "Valid number: 42</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "abc",
        "Invalid number</pre>",
        "<p>An <code>else</code> block after <code>try</code>/<code>except</code>",
        "runs only when the <code>try</code> block completed with",
        "<strong>no</strong> exception &mdash; it separates \"what happens on",
        "success\" from the risky code itself.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "try:",
        "    n = int(s)",
        "except ValueError:",
        "    print(\"Invalid number\")",
        "# TODO: add an else block that prints an f-string: Valid number: {n}"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "42"
          ],
          "expected": "Valid number: 42"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "abc"
          ],
          "expected": "Invalid number"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Valid number: 0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "04",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "The finally Clause",
      "sidebar_tag": "finally",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a line of text and try converting it to an integer, printing",
        "it if it works or <code>Invalid number</code> if it doesn't &mdash;",
        "then, no matter which branch ran, print <code>Done</code> on the",
        "line after, using a <code>finally</code> block.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "42",
        "42",
        "Done</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "abc",
        "Invalid number",
        "Done</pre>",
        "<p>Code inside <code>finally</code> always runs, whether the",
        "<code>try</code> block succeeded or failed &mdash; it's meant for",
        "cleanup work that has to happen either way.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "try:",
        "    print(int(s))",
        "except ValueError:",
        "    print(\"Invalid number\")",
        "# TODO: add a finally block that prints \"Done\""
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "42"
          ],
          "expected": "42\nDone"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "abc"
          ],
          "expected": "Invalid number\nDone"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "05",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Catching Multiple Exception Types Together",
      "sidebar_tag": "except (A, B)",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read two lines of text meant to represent numbers, and try",
        "converting both to integers and printing their quotient. This time,",
        "a <strong>single</strong> <code>except</code> should catch two",
        "different problems at once: a bad number, or a division by zero.",
        "Print <code>Invalid input</code> for either one.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "10",
        "2",
        "5.0</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "abc",
        "2",
        "Invalid input</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "10",
        "0",
        "Invalid input</pre>",
        "<p>Putting multiple exception types in parentheses after",
        "<code>except</code> catches either one with the same block, for",
        "cases where they should both be handled the same way.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "a_str = input()",
        "b_str = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: try converting a_str and b_str to ints and printing their quotient.",
        "# Catch (ValueError, ZeroDivisionError) together and print \"Invalid input\""
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "5.0"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "abc",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "Invalid input"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Invalid input"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "6",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "2.0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "06",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Separate Messages for Separate Problems",
      "sidebar_tag": "multiple except",
      "description": [
        "<p>Same setup as the last exercise &mdash; read two lines meant to be",
        "numbers and try dividing them &mdash; but this time use",
        "<strong>two separate</strong> <code>except</code> blocks, since a",
        "bad number and a division by zero deserve different messages:",
        "<code>Not a valid number</code> for the first,",
        "<code>Cannot divide by zero</code> for the second.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "abc",
        "2",
        "Not a valid number</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "10",
        "0",
        "Cannot divide by zero</pre>",
        "<p>Python checks a chain of <code>except</code> blocks top to",
        "bottom, just like an <code>elif</code> chain, and runs the first",
        "one whose exception type matches.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "a_str = input()",
        "b_str = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: try converting a_str and b_str to ints and printing their quotient.",
        "# Use a separate except block for ValueError (\"Not a valid number\")",
        "# and ZeroDivisionError (\"Cannot divide by zero\")"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "5.0"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "abc",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "Not a valid number"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Cannot divide by zero"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "07",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Looping Until the Input Is Valid",
      "sidebar_tag": "try + while",
      "description": [
        "<p>Keep asking for a number until the person enters one that",
        "actually works. Read a line of text; if <code>int()</code> raises a",
        "<code>ValueError</code>, print <code>Try again</code> and read",
        "another line. Once a valid number arrives, print",
        "<code>Got it: &lt;n&gt;</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "abc",
        "Try again",
        "xyz",
        "Try again",
        "7",
        "Got it: 7</pre>",
        "<p>This is the same \"keep reading until it's good enough\" shape",
        "from an earlier <code>while</code>-loop exercise &mdash; except now",
        "the input itself might crash the program, so the loop needs a",
        "<code>try</code>/<code>except</code> around each attempt.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "while True:",
        "    s = input()",
        "    # TODO: try converting s to an int.",
        "    # If it works, print \"Got it: <n>\" and stop looping.",
        "    # If it raises a ValueError, print \"Try again\" and loop again."
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "abc",
            "xyz",
            "7"
          ],
          "expected": "Try again\nTry again\nGot it: 7"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "5"
          ],
          "expected": "Got it: 5"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "1.5",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "Try again\nGot it: 3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "08",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Raising Your Own Exception",
      "sidebar_tag": "raise",
      "description": [
        "<p>Define a function <code>check_age(age)</code> that",
        "<strong>raises</strong> a <code>ValueError</code> if <code>age</code>",
        "is negative, and otherwise does nothing. In the main part of the",
        "program, read a number, call <code>check_age()</code> inside a",
        "<code>try</code> block, and print <code>Valid age</code> if it",
        "didn't raise, or <code>Invalid age</code> if it did.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "16",
        "Valid age</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "-5",
        "Invalid age</pre>",
        "<p>Exceptions aren't only raised automatically by things like",
        "<code>int()</code> &mdash; your own code can <code>raise</code> one",
        "too, as a way of saying \"something here is invalid\" and letting",
        "whoever called your function decide how to handle it.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "def check_age(age):",
        "    # TODO: if age < 0, raise a ValueError",
        "",
        "age = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: call check_age(age) inside a try block.",
        "# Print \"Valid age\" if nothing goes wrong, or \"Invalid age\"",
        "# if a ValueError is raised."
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "16"
          ],
          "expected": "Valid age"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "-5"
          ],
          "expected": "Invalid age"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Valid age"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "09",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Catching Your Own Message",
      "sidebar_tag": "except ... as e",
      "description": [
        "<p>Define a function <code>check_age(age)</code> that raises",
        "<code>ValueError(\"Age cannot be negative\")</code> if <code>age</code>",
        "is negative. Read a number, call the function inside a",
        "<code>try</code> block, and if it raises, catch it with",
        "<code>as e</code> and print the exception's message directly with",
        "<code>print(e)</code>. Otherwise, print <code>Valid age</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "-5",
        "Age cannot be negative</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "16",
        "Valid age</pre>",
        "<p>Writing text inside <code>raise ValueError(\"...\")</code>",
        "attaches a message to the exception &mdash; catching it with",
        "<code>except ValueError as e</code> gives you access to that",
        "message through the variable <code>e</code>.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "def check_age(age):",
        "    # TODO: if age < 0, raise ValueError(\"Age cannot be negative\")",
        "",
        "age = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: call check_age(age) inside a try block.",
        "# If it raises, catch it as e and print e.",
        "# Otherwise, print \"Valid age\""
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "-5"
          ],
          "expected": "Age cannot be negative"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "16"
          ],
          "expected": "Valid age"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Valid age"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Catching Any Exception",
      "sidebar_tag": "except Exception",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a line of text and a divisor on the next line. Try converting",
        "the text to an integer and dividing it by the divisor, printing the",
        "result. This time, use a single <code>except Exception:</code> to",
        "catch <strong>anything</strong> that goes wrong &mdash; a bad number",
        "or a division by zero alike &mdash; and print",
        "<code>Something went wrong</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "10",
        "2",
        "5.0</pre>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "abc",
        "2",
        "Something went wrong</pre>",
        "<p><code>Exception</code> is the base class nearly every built-in",
        "error inherits from, so catching it catches almost anything. It's",
        "convenient, but it's also a blunt tool &mdash; it can't tell you",
        "<em>which</em> problem occurred, which is why the more specific",
        "<code>except</code> blocks from earlier exercises are usually the",
        "better choice when you know what might go wrong.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "divisor = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: try converting s to an int and dividing it by divisor, then print the result.",
        "# Catch any Exception and print \"Something went wrong\""
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "5.0"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "abc",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "Something went wrong"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Something went wrong"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Fix the Bug",
      "sidebar_tag": "debug",
      "description": [
        "<p>This code is supposed to print <code>Cannot divide by zero</code>",
        "when dividing by zero, and <code>Invalid input</code> when the first",
        "value isn't a valid number. Run it with a non-numeric first value,",
        "like <code>abc</code>, and see what happens.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "a = input()",
        "b = int(input())",
        "",
        "try:",
        "    print(int(a) / b)",
        "except ZeroDivisionError:",
        "    print(\"Cannot divide by zero\")"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "5.0"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Cannot divide by zero"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "abc",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "Invalid input"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "source_unit": "try-except",
      "title": "Fix the Bug",
      "sidebar_tag": "debug",
      "description": [
        "<p>This code is supposed to print the specific message",
        "<code>Age cannot be negative</code> when given a negative age, using",
        "the same custom-exception setup as an earlier exercise. Run it with",
        "a negative age and see what actually prints instead.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "def check_age(age):",
        "    if age < 0:",
        "        raise ValueError(\"Age cannot be negative\")",
        "",
        "age = int(input())",
        "",
        "try:",
        "    check_age(age)",
        "    print(\"Valid age\")",
        "except Exception:",
        "    print(\"Something went wrong\")",
        "except ValueError as e:",
        "    print(e)"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "-5"
          ],
          "expected": "Age cannot be negative"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "16"
          ],
          "expected": "Valid age"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "Valid age"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
